.\" Checked against aureole/aureole.nim, startup/, runtime/, model/ and x11/.
.TH AUREOLE 1 "November 22, 2026" CaelWM "CaelWM Manual"
.SH NAME
aureole \- CaelWM X11 window manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aureole
.RB [ \-c " CONFIG_PATH"]
.br
.B aureole \-h
.br
.B aureole \-v
.br
.B aureole \-\-abi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B aureole
is the Nim X11 window-manager core of CaelWM. It owns the selected X screen's
window-management event mask and manages client lifecycle, focus, stacking,
geometry, decorations, groups, pointer actions, and EWMH/ICCCM integration
through XCB. Startup fails if another window manager owns the screen.
.PP
.BR borealis (1)
sends low-level commands directly to Aureole over X11.
.BR aether (1)
provides workflow, layout, rule and saved-state policy.
.BR aetherd (1)
observes WM snapshots and X11 lifecycle changes, maintains controller state
and applies application rules. Panels, application autostart and keyboard
bindings are supplied by the session configuration, rather than a built-in
desktop shell.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP 7
.B \-h
Print short usage to standard error and exit 0 without connecting to X11.
.TP
.B \-v
Print the compiled version and license notice to standard error and exit 0
without connecting to X11. The source default version is 0.5.2; the build can
override it.
.TP
.B \-c CONFIG_PATH
Execute this startup script instead of the default aureolerc. An attached
argument such as \-c/path/to/aureolerc is also accepted. If repeated, the last
parsed value applies. A relative script path is resolved from the current
directory.
.TP
.B \-\-
Stop option processing; subsequent arguments are ignored.
.PP
Unrecognized options and a missing \-c argument produce diagnostics but do not
by themselves abort normal startup. Positional arguments are ignored. There
are no supported long help, version or configuration aliases.
.SS Diagnostic entrypoints
These implementation diagnostics are separate from normal startup. They can
claim WM ownership and change event processing; they are not read-only
inspection commands for a running desktop.
.TP
.B \-\-abi
Print the compiled X event ABI report to standard output and exit before
connecting to X11.
.TP
.B \-\-test\-events
Log normalized events. Used alone, run the event observer without normal
lifecycle dispatch.
.TP
.B \-\-test\-state
Print startup state. Without a stage4, stage5 or stage6 mode, leave startup
adoption observation-only.
.TP
.B \-\-test\-lifecycle
Enable lifecycle tracing.
.TP
.B \-\-test\-pause\-before\-effects
Enable lifecycle tracing and pause before effect realization.
.TP
.B \-\-test\-stage4
Enable lifecycle tracing and test controls.
.TP
.B \-\-test\-stage5
Enable lifecycle tracing, state output, test controls and geometry
diagnostics.
.TP
.B \-\-test\-stage6
Enable lifecycle tracing, test controls and stacking diagnostics.
.TP
.B \-\-test\-private\-effects
Requires a special private-execution build. Rejected with status 2 by the
ordinary Makefile build.
.PP
The test modes suppress the normal aureolerc launch and EWMH startup
publication path. Their combinations are developer interfaces, not session
configuration options.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The default startup script is
.BR ~/.config/aureole/aureolerc .
If XDG_CONFIG_HOME exists in the environment, the default is instead
.BR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/aureole/aureolerc ,
even when its value is empty. An explicit nonempty \-c path takes precedence.
With neither XDG_CONFIG_HOME nor HOME set, the script is skipped.
.PP
The file is an executable program, typically a shell script with a shebang,
not a declarative file parsed by the WM. Aureole checks that it exists and has
an execute permission bit, then launches it asynchronously using setsid found
through PATH, inheriting the environment and standard streams. It passes no
script arguments and does not wait for configuration to finish before adopting
clients and servicing events. The XCB connection descriptor is closed on
execution in the child.
.PP
Missing or nonexecutable scripts, unavailable setsid, launch failures and
child exit statuses are diagnosed; these do not normally terminate the WM. On
shutdown, a still-running startup process group receives SIGTERM, followed by
SIGKILL if it remains running after about 750 milliseconds.
.PP
Use
.B borealis wm config KEY VALUE ...
inside the script to set the supported runtime settings documented in
.BR borealis (1).
The supplied session's script configures borders, gaps, groups, focus and
mouse behavior, initializes filesystem state and invokes its autostart helper.
Its recipe and autostart helpers are session dependencies, not Aureole
configuration syntax. Aureole does not implement a signal-based configuration
reload.
.SH STARTUP AND AETHERD
Normal startup connects to X11, claims WM ownership, publishes WM identity and
initial EWMH state, initializes RandR, launches aureolerc, adopts eligible
existing windows, completes management, establishes focus and publishes client
lists before entering the event loop.
.PP
Startup adoption excludes override-redirect windows and dock, toolbar and
desktop window types. Viewable eligible windows are adopted; an unmapped
window is adopted when its WM_STATE is Normal and its desktop property names a
valid group. Valid desktop assignments are retained; otherwise the startup
default is group 1 when available.
.PP
Aureole itself does not spawn, stop or wait for Aetherd. The prepared
.B config/aureole-session.sh
fragment, sourced by the au* branch of the surrounding .xinitrc, provisions
the session environment, starts Aetherd with output appended to WMLOG, starts
sxhkd, and invokes Aureole through xexec. The fragment checks for an existing
WM/controller before starting the session. Its ENV helper initializes the
runtime tree; it is not a configuration reload mechanism.
.PP
Aetherd can start before the WM and retry attachment. Controller readiness is
observed with
.BR "aether daemon await" .
The retained controller endpoint is $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/zephyr/zephyrd.sock, or
$WME/zephyrd.sock when XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is empty. This socket belongs to
Aetherd; Aureole's Borealis command channel uses X11 properties and
ClientMessage events.
.SH WINDOWS AND GROUPS
The WM owns window identity, membership, visibility, focus and stacking state.
Ordinary new clients normally inherit a valid focused group, falling back to
group 1; sticky_windows assigns new clients to the ungrouped state. Hidden and
inactive-group clients remain managed until their actual lifecycle ends.
.PP
The default group-slot count is 10. The public Aether workflow uses numeric
groups 1 through count minus 1. Borealis passes numbers unchanged to the WM,
which also accepts slot 0 for several low-level actions; there is no
one-based-to-zero-based subtraction. The internal ungrouped value is
0xffffffff (4294967295), distinct from slot 0. Raw observations may report
this sentinel.
.PP
Multiple groups may be active simultaneously. Activating a populated inactive
group maps its members but does not select focus or change the current group
merely by activation. The current group is derived from the focused managed
window; without one it is the ungrouped sentinel. Deactivating the focused
group clears focus and unmaps its members. To choose a window after
activation, issue an explicit focus command or use Aether's group workflow.
.PP
Geometry commands and persistent normal, above and overlay layers are exposed
through Borealis. Maximized and monocled clients participate in a separate
zoomed stacking tier below overlay. Layout policies such as fold and explode
belong to Aether.
.SH RESTART AND EXIT
.B borealis wm restart
requests WM cleanup and exit status 75; Aureole does not exec itself. Restart
cleanup detaches save-set clients and explicitly preserves hidden mapping
state before closing the X connection.
.PP
.B borealis wm quit STATUS
requests termination with the supplied status. The IPC word is interpreted as
signed 32-bit by the WM; normal shell exit-status truncation applies.
Supplying 75 to quit is not the same internal cleanup path as restart, though
a surrounding supervisor may react to that status in the same way.
.PP
The prepared .xinitrc xexec loop launches the same command again only for
status 75; other statuses break that loop. This restart policy and subsequent
X session termination belong to the wrapper and X session, not the WM. The
wrapper does not restart Aetherd for each WM restart.
.PP
SIGTERM and SIGINT wake and stop the event loop. That path normally returns 0
and closes the startup child and X connection; it does not request the
restart-specific cleanup path. Caught startup or runtime exceptions are
reported and return 1. Errors rejected by diagnostic-mode selection can return
2.
.SH ENVIRONMENT AND FILES
.TP
.B DISPLAY, XAUTHORITY
Select and authorize the X11 connection. No command-line display selector is
implemented.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME, HOME
Determine the startup script path as described above.
.TP
.B PATH
Used to find setsid and inherited by aureolerc and its helpers.
.TP
.B ~/.config/aureole/aureolerc
Default executable startup configuration.
.TP
.B ~/.config/aureole/ENV, ~/.session/aureole
Environment helper and persistent settings used by the prepared session, not
files read directly by the WM core.
.TP
.B WME, GROUP, HIDDEN, WINFO, WMSE, FIFO, WMLOG
Session/controller paths inherited by helpers. The prepared ENV sets WME to
/dev/shm/$WM, GROUP to $WME/group, HIDDEN to $WME/hidden, WINFO to
$WME/window, WMSE to $HOME/.session/$WM, FIFO to $WME/fifo, and WMLOG to
/tmp/$WM.log. Aureole does not itself maintain the controller's filesystem
mirror.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Normal startup and configuration-child diagnostics go to standard error. The
WM does not open a log file itself; the prepared supervisor redirects output
to $ROOT/aureole.log, with ROOT normally /tmp.
.PP
Test flags add READY, EVENT, STATE and lifecycle messages. Retained diagnostic
environment names include CIRRUS_STAGE5_TRACE=1 for geometry-effect traces and
CIRRUS_NIM_LIFECYCLE_INVALIDATION_TRACE=1 for status/invalidation traces.
Additional fault-injection and private-build controls are implementation test
facilities, not normal configuration. Some X11 atoms retain CIRRUS or __WM_IPC
names for compatibility.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR borealis (1),
.BR aether (1),
.BR aetherd (1)
